B. Steady-state output failure per problem = p_f0 * w * f_eff (problem-specific insights: does not decay)
   p_f0 = first-run failure rate on a new problem, w = share of failed runs with a claimed solution, f_eff = verifier pass rate after reruns
    p_f0    w | f=0.02  f=0.10  f=0.20  f=0.35  f=0.50 
     0.1  0.2 |    0.0%    0.2%    0.4%    0.7%    1.0%
     0.1  0.5 |    0.1%    0.5%    1.0%    1.8%    2.5%
     0.1  0.8 |    0.2%    0.8%    1.6%    2.8%    4.0%
     0.3  0.2 |    0.1%    0.6%    1.2%    2.1%    3.0%
     0.3  0.5 |    0.3%    1.5%    3.0%    5.2%    7.5%
     0.3  0.8 |    0.5%    2.4%    4.8%    8.4%   12.0%
     0.5  0.2 |    0.2%    1.0%    2.0%    3.5%    5.0%
     0.5  0.5 |    0.5%    2.5%    5.0%    8.8%   12.5%
     0.5  0.8 |    0.8%    4.0%    8.0%   14.0%   20.0%
   'Works' needs <= 5%.

C. Enrichment: share of verifier-targeting (malign) deviations among accepted wrong outputs
   prior odds r = malign : honest among deviations reaching the output; f_m = 0.9 (targets V's blind spots)
          r | f_h=0.3   f_h=0.1   f_h=0.01  f_h=0.001
        0.1 |       23%       47%       90%       99%
       0.01 |        3%        8%       47%       90%
      0.001 |        0%        1%        8%       47%
   bits of enrichment log2(f_m/f_h): f_h=0.3: 1.6, f_h=0.1: 3.2, f_h=0.01: 6.5, f_h=0.001: 9.8
   A better honest verifier makes the accepted errors *more* adversarial, unless f_m falls with f_h.

D. Bits of selection
   Deterministic decoder, reruns only after a rejection: per problem <= log2(1+R_p);
   lifetime max over sum R_p <= K (b_alpha = 1) is T*log2(1+K/T) <= K*log2(e)
   K=100: T=10: 35 bits (3.46 per problem); T=100: 100 bits (1.00 per problem); T=1000: 138 bits (0.14 per problem)
   K=1000: T=100: 346 bits (3.46 per problem); T=1000: 1000 bits (1.00 per problem); T=10000: 1375 bits (0.14 per problem)
   Resampling decoder (best-of-k, no update between attempts): KL(output || policy) = ln k - (k-1)/k
   k=2: 0.28 bits, k=4: 0.92 bits, k=16: 2.65 bits, k=64: 4.58 bits, k=256: 6.56 bits, k=1024: 8.56 bits

E. Heavy tails: phi = Pr[tau(x0) > n] with n = 2 n0 and n0 at quantile q
   q=0.50 | lognormal s=0.75: 17.8%, lognormal s=1.00: 24.4%, lognormal s=1.25: 29.0% | Pareto a=1.2: 21.8%, Pareto a=1.5: 17.7%, Pareto a=2.0: 12.5%
   q=0.75 | lognormal s=0.75:  5.5%, lognormal s=1.00:  8.6%, lognormal s=1.25: 11.0% | Pareto a=1.2: 10.9%, Pareto a=1.5:  8.8%, Pareto a=2.0:  6.2%
   q=0.90 | lognormal s=0.75:  1.4%, lognormal s=1.00:  2.4%, lognormal s=1.25:  3.3% | Pareto a=1.2:  4.4%, Pareto a=1.5:  3.5%, Pareto a=2.0:  2.5%

   Effort budget: VSI <= CI/5 = 4 n0 K at eps = 5%. VSI effort / (n0 K) =
     a*iota (search, a = 3 worst case) + 2*iota*phi*(T/K) (futile full search of n on
     each problem the human's own chain cannot finish) + beta*(T/K)/n0 (audits)
     + c_V*(T/K + 1)/n0 (verifications, c_V task steps each).  n0 = 30, beta = 1.
   Largest T/K for which VSI still meets the budget ('-' = never):
     a  iota   c_V | phi=0.00  phi=0.05  phi=0.10  phi=0.20 
     1   1.0   0.1 |      81.7      21.9      12.7       6.9
     1   1.0   0.5 |      59.7      19.9      11.9       6.6
     1   1.3   0.1 |      73.5      16.2       9.1       4.8
     1   1.3   0.5 |      53.7      14.9       8.7       4.7
     1   1.6   0.1 |      65.4      12.2       6.7       3.5
     1   1.6   0.5 |      47.7      11.3       6.4       3.5
     2   1.0   0.1 |      54.5      14.6       8.4       4.6
     2   1.0   0.5 |      39.7      13.2       7.9       4.4
     2   1.3   0.1 |      38.1       8.4       4.7       2.5
     2   1.3   0.5 |      27.7       7.7       4.5       2.4
     2   1.6   0.1 |      21.7       4.1       2.2       1.2
     2   1.6   0.5 |      15.7       3.7       2.1       1.1
     3   1.0   0.1 |      27.2       7.3       4.2       2.3
     3   1.0   0.5 |      19.7       6.6       3.9       2.2
     3   1.3   0.1 |       2.6       0.6       0.3       0.2
     3   1.3   0.5 |       1.7       0.5       0.3       0.1
     3   1.6   0.1 |         -         -         -         -
     3   1.6   0.5 |         -         -         -         -
   With a = 3 the worst case alone fits only if iota < 4/3.

   Against the human doing every problem itself (about n0 per problem): on each problem
   its own chain cannot finish within n, VSI ends with the human answering every step of
   a full run (>= n = 2*iota*n0 task queries) and no output, so the saving is at most
   about 1/(2*iota*phi), before any other cost:
     iota=1.0: phi=0.05: 10.0x, phi=0.10: 5.0x, phi=0.20: 2.5x
     iota=1.3: phi=0.05: 7.7x, phi=0.10: 3.8x, phi=0.20: 1.9x
     iota=1.6: phi=0.05: 6.2x, phi=0.10: 3.1x, phi=0.20: 1.6x
